          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Painting a concrete floor yourself typically costs roughly $0.30 to $0.80 per square foot in materials, plus about $50 to $100 in tools, so a 500-square-foot basement usually runs about $200 to $500. Hiring a professional typically costs roughly $2 to $5 per square foot including basic prep and two coats, or about $1,000 to $2,500 for the same basement, and small jobs often hit a contractor's minimum charge. Those are typical US ranges that vary by region and by how much prep the slab needs, and prices change over time. Paint is cheaper up front than epoxy or a stain-and-seal finish, but it usually needs recoating sooner, so the cheapest choice depends on how long you want the floor to last.
        </p>

        <div id="short" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Much Does It Cost to Paint a Concrete Floor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most homeowners the answer comes down to labor. The materials for a painted concrete floor are inexpensive: a gallon of quality porch and floor paint or one-part epoxy-acrylic garage floor paint commonly costs roughly $35 to $70 and covers around 300 to 400 square feet per coat on smooth concrete. Two coats, a degreaser and an etcher add up to well under a dollar per square foot. A professional crew charges mainly for the time it takes to prepare the slab properly, which is the part that decides whether the paint lasts.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical US Ranges</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$0.30-0.80</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">DIY materials per sq ft</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2-5</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Professional, per sq ft</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$200-500</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">DIY, 500 sq ft basement</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1,000-2,500</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pro, 500 sq ft basement</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The spread inside each range has clear reasons. On the DIY side, it is mostly paint quality and whether you need a primer. On the professional side, it is prep: a clean, dry, previously unpainted slab that only needs degreasing and etching sits at the low end, while a floor with old peeling paint that has to be ground off, cracks to repair or a moisture problem to address can push past the top of the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            These figures are for a painted finish, meaning latex, acrylic or one-part epoxy-acrylic floor paint. Two-part epoxy, polyaspartic and decorative stain finishes cost more and are covered in the comparison section below. If you have not chosen a paint yet, our <a href="/best-concrete-floor-paint/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best concrete floor paint</a> guide compares the products we recommend for basements, porches and garages, which sit at different points in that $35 to $70 per gallon range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Does It Cost to Paint a Basement or Garage Floor by Size?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The table below applies the per-square-foot ranges to common floors. DIY figures include two coats of paint, degreaser, etcher and basic tools, but not a primer, grinder rental or crack repair. Professional figures assume basic prep and two coats on a sound, dry slab. Real quotes vary by region, contractor and floor condition, and small floors often cost more per square foot because of minimum charges.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Floor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx. size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">DIY materials and tools</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Professional</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Porch or small patio</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $110-$260</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $400-$1,000 (often a minimum charge)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">One-car garage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">250 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $125-$300</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $500-$1,250</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two-car garage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">450 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $185-$460</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $900-$2,250</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Basement, partial</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">500 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $200-$500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1,000-$2,500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Basement, full</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,000 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $350-$900</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2,000-$5,000</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Garage sizes vary more than people expect. A one-car garage is often around 12 by 20 feet, while two-car garages range from about 20 by 20 to 24 by 24 feet, roughly 400 to 575 square feet. Measure your own floor and multiply. Subtract nothing for areas under shelving or the water heater if you plan to paint around them later; those spots cost labor even when they use little paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Basements are where the size changes the math the most. A 1,000-square-foot open basement is a straightforward DIY project over a weekend or two, and the materials stay cheap. The same floor hired out becomes a meaningful expense, which is why many homeowners do basements themselves and hire out garages, where hot tires and oil make prep quality more critical.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are pricing a garage specifically and weighing coatings rather than paint, our <a href="/garage-floor-coating-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">garage floor coating cost</a> guide and our <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">epoxy garage floor cost</a> breakdown cover the more expensive systems in detail. Paint is the budget end of that spectrum and the right choice for many floors, but not all of them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Does DIY Concrete Floor Painting Cost, Item by Item?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A DIY job breaks into consumables you buy per square foot, tools you buy once and optional extras that depend on the slab. The table shows typical retail ranges. Store-brand and premium products sit at opposite ends, and the paint itself is the line where spending more usually pays off, because higher-quality floor paints tend to wear and adhere better.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical cost</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Floor paint, per gallon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $35-$70</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Covers roughly 300-400 sq ft per coat on smooth concrete</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete primer, per gallon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $30-$55</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Needed on some floors; check the paint label</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Degreaser</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $10-$25</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">More on oily garage floors</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Citric or acid etcher</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $15-$35</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Per 250-500 sq ft, depending on product</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Crack filler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $10-$25 per tube or bottle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Depends on crack width and length</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rollers, tray, brush, pole, tape</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $40-$80</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One-time purchase</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Anti-slip additive</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $10-$25</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Optional; for patios, steps, wet areas</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Floor grinder rental</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $100-$250 per day</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Optional; plus diamond wheel wear and a vacuum</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For a typical two-coat job, the paint is about $0.18 to $0.47 per square foot at those prices and spread rates. Add a primer and the paint-plus-primer cost rises by roughly $0.08 to $0.20 per square foot. Degreaser and etcher add a few cents per square foot. That is how a basic DIY job lands around $0.30 to $0.80 per square foot plus tools, and how a fully primed, ground floor can approach a dollar or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rough or porous concrete drinks more paint. A broom-finished patio or an old, worn basement slab can drop coverage to around 200 to 300 square feet per gallon per coat, which raises the paint cost by a third or more. Buy for the low end of the spread rate on the label and return unopened cans if you have extras. Running out halfway through a coat and mixing in a different batch risks a visible color shift.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your time is the other DIY cost. Expect one day for cleaning, degreasing and etching, at least a day for the slab to dry, then a day for primer and paint with drying time between coats. Grinding adds a day. Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-paint-a-concrete-floor/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to paint a concrete floor</a> lays out the sequence so you can plan the weekend realistically.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Do Professionals Charge to Paint a Concrete Floor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Professional concrete floor painting typically runs roughly $2 to $5 per square foot for basic prep and two coats of floor paint on a sound slab. Painting contractors, handymen and some flooring and coating companies all do this work, and pricing varies with who you hire. A handyman may charge less for a small basement; a coating company that specializes in garages may quote paint only reluctantly and steer you toward epoxy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Minimum charges shape small jobs. Many contractors have a minimum that covers travel, setup and cleanup, often several hundred dollars, so a 200-square-foot porch might cost as much as a much larger floor would at a per-square-foot rate. Garage coating companies commonly have higher minimums than painters. Bundling the floor with other painting in the house is one of the easiest ways to get around a minimum.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prep is where quotes diverge. A low quote often means degreasing and etching only. A higher quote may include grinding, crack repair, a moisture test and primer. The higher quote is not automatically a better deal, but on a garage floor or a slab with any history of peeling, the extra prep is usually what you are paying for. Ask each contractor to describe exactly how they will prepare the surface, which our section on fair quotes below covers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Removing old coatings is the most common reason a professional quote jumps. If the floor has peeling paint or a failed coating, the contractor may need to strip or grind it all off before starting, and that work can add roughly $1 to $3 per square foot depending on what is there and how well it is stuck. If you are dealing with a failed floor, our guide to <a href="/why-is-my-concrete-floor-paint-peeling/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">why concrete floor paint peels</a> helps you understand what the contractor is fixing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Paint Cheaper Than Epoxy or Stain?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Up front, yes. Floor paint is the cheapest way to color concrete. Two-part epoxy and stain-and-seal finishes cost more in materials and usually in labor, and full broadcast-chip or polyaspartic systems cost several times more. The table puts the typical ranges side by side, along with how long each finish usually lasts before it needs significant work.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Finish</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">DIY per sq ft</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pro per sq ft</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical life before recoat</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acrylic or 1-part epoxy-acrylic floor paint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.30-$0.80</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2-$5</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-5 years; 1-3 in a busy garage</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Penetrating stain plus sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.50-$1.50</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2-$6 (more for decorative)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Color lasts; reseal every 1-3 years outdoors</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DIY two-part epoxy kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.50-$3</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not applicable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Several years with good prep</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Professional epoxy system</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not applicable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $3-$8</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 5-8 years or more</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polyaspartic or full chip system</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not applicable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $8-$12</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often 10 years or more</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Lifetime cost tells a different story. A painted two-car garage that needs repainting every two years can, over a decade, add up to several DIY jobs and several weekends, while a professional epoxy system might go most of that decade with only cleaning. For a basement with foot traffic only, the opposite is true: a well-applied floor paint can last many years and stays far cheaper than any coating system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stain sits in between. Materials cost more than paint because you buy both stain and sealer, but a stained floor cannot peel, and maintenance is a quick reseal rather than a repaint. On a damp-prone slab, where paint would fail early, stain is often the cheapest finish over time even though it costs more on day one. Our <a href="/concrete-stain-vs-paint/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete stain vs paint</a> comparison explains which slabs suit which finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            For garages, read our <a href="/garage-floor-paint-vs-epoxy/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">garage floor paint vs epoxy</a> comparison before deciding on price alone. If you park on the floor every day, paint's lower upfront cost can be wiped out by hot-tire damage and repeated recoating. If the garage is a workshop with occasional parking, paint is often the sensible budget choice, and our <a href="/best-garage-floor-paint/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">garage floor paint picks</a> cover the products that hold up best.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hidden" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Hidden Costs Should You Budget For?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Moisture problems are the big one, because they can make the paint itself a waste of money. If a plastic sheet test shows moisture coming through the slab, the real cost is fixing drainage, gutters, grading or cracks first, or choosing a breathable finish instead. Those fixes can range from a few dollars for downspout extensions to thousands for interior drainage. Run the test before you buy paint; our <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-moisture-test/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete moisture test guide</a> explains how.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crack and surface repair add up on older slabs. A few hairline cracks cost a tube of filler. Wide cracks, spalled areas or uneven sections need patching compound or epoxy repair products and more labor. Professionals usually price repairs per linear foot or as an add-on. Our <a href="/best-concrete-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete crack filler</a> guide helps you match products to the cracks you have.
          </p>

          <p>
            Removing old paint or sealer is the second big hidden cost. Paint will not bond to an old sealer or to paint that is already peeling, so both have to come off. For DIY, that may mean a grinder rental, diamond wheels and a dust extractor, often $150 to $300 or more for a weekend. For a pro, it is the add-on mentioned above. If the old paint could be from before 1978, a lead test kit is a small but necessary extra.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smaller extras round out the budget: a primer when the label requires it, anti-slip additive for outdoor and wet areas, a dehumidifier or heater to get a basement into the label's temperature and humidity range, moving and storing the room's contents, and a few days without the use of a garage. None of these are large on their own, but together they can add 20 to 30 percent to a DIY materials budget.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="quote" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Should You DIY, and How Do You Get a Fair Quote?
          </h2>

          <p>
            DIY makes the most sense for basements, porches and workshop floors where foot traffic is the main load, the slab is dry and the old surface is bare or sound. You save most of the cost, and the work is physical but not highly skilled. It makes less sense when the floor has a failed coating to remove, a moisture issue or daily vehicle traffic, because those jobs depend on prep equipment and experience that are expensive to rent and learn.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you hire, get at least three written quotes and compare them line by line rather than by total. Each quote should name the exact product and color, the number of coats, and the prep method, whether that is etching, grinding or both. It should say whether the contractor will test for moisture, how cracks will be repaired and whether a primer is included. A quote that just says paint floor, two coats is not comparable to one that spells out grinding, crack repair and primer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask about timing and warranty. A reputable contractor will tell you how long before you can walk on the floor and how long before you can park, typically about a day and about a week for floor paint. Ask what the warranty covers, especially peeling, and whether it excludes moisture failures, which many do. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-does-concrete-paint-take-to-dry/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long concrete paint takes to dry</a> gives you the numbers to check a contractor's schedule against.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be wary of quotes far below the range. A price well under $2 per square foot for a professional job usually means minimal prep, a lower-grade paint or a single coat, and on concrete, those shortcuts show up as peeling within a year or two. Prices change and vary by region, so treat the ranges on this page as a sanity check, not a substitute for local quotes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Budgeting only for paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint is often less than half the cost of a DIY job once degreaser, etcher, primer, crack filler and tools are included. On floors with old coatings, a grinder rental can cost more than the paint. Price the full list before starting so a missing primer or etcher does not push you into skipping a step that matters.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the cheapest quote blindly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Low quotes usually save money on prep, which is the one thing that decides whether floor paint lasts. Compare how each contractor will clean, profile, repair and prime the slab. A quote that skips grinding or a moisture test on a garage or basement floor is often the most expensive one once the floor starts peeling.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring a failed moisture test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint on a slab that passes moisture vapor is likely to blister and peel, so every dollar spent on it is at risk. Fixing drainage or choosing a breathable stain and sealer can cost more up front but less over time. Test before buying paint, not after the first coat starts lifting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underbuying paint for rough concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Porous or broom-finished concrete can absorb far more paint than the label's best-case coverage. Running short mid-coat forces a second batch that may not match exactly. Measure the floor, use the low end of the label's spread rate, and box multiple cans together so the color stays consistent across the whole floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing only upfront cost with epoxy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Floor paint is cheaper on day one, but in a garage with daily parking it may need repainting every year or two. Add up the expected recoats, materials and weekends over five to ten years before deciding. In a basement with foot traffic only, paint usually wins on lifetime cost as well as upfront.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many gallons of paint do I need for a concrete floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide the floor area by the label's spread rate, often around 300 to 400 square feet per gallon on smooth concrete, then multiply by two coats. A 500-square-foot basement typically needs about three to four gallons total. Rough or porous concrete may need more. Buy a little extra so you can do touch-ups later from the same batch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it cheaper to paint or epoxy a garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Painting is cheaper up front: roughly $0.30 to $0.80 per square foot DIY versus about $0.50 to $3 for a DIY epoxy kit, and roughly $2 to $5 versus $3 to $8 per square foot professionally. But floor paint usually needs recoating sooner in a garage with daily parking, so epoxy can cost less over the life of the floor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to paint a 2-car garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical two-car garage of about 450 square feet, expect roughly $185 to $460 for DIY materials and tools, or about $900 to $2,250 professionally with basic prep. Larger two-car garages around 575 square feet cost proportionally more. Removing old coatings, repairing cracks or grinding the slab can add significantly to either figure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does painting a basement floor add value to a home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A clean, freshly painted basement floor makes the space look finished and cared for, which can help a home show better, but it is unlikely to add value equal to more expensive flooring. It is best seen as an inexpensive cosmetic upgrade. Peeling or blistered paint can hurt impressions, so only paint a basement floor that passes a moisture test.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do you need to repaint a concrete floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on traffic and conditions. A dry basement with foot traffic can go several years, often three to five or more. Covered porches commonly last two to four years. Garage floors with daily parking may need touch-ups or repainting every year or two. Good prep and sweeping regularly to remove grit both extend the time between repaints.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it cheaper to stain or paint a concrete floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Paint is usually cheaper to apply, with DIY materials around $0.30 to $0.80 per square foot compared with about $0.50 to $1.50 for stain plus sealer. Over time the gap can close, because stain cannot peel and only needs periodic resealing. On damp slabs, where paint tends to fail early, stain is often the cheaper long-term choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to hire a pro to paint a garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Most homeowners can paint a garage floor with a one-part floor paint if the slab is dry and bare or soundly painted. Hiring a pro makes more sense when old coatings must be ground off, cracks need serious repair or you want a two-part epoxy or polyaspartic system, which need more equipment and experience to install well.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

